<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yury of Zvenigorod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1374">1374</a>–<a href="/years/1434">1434</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Yury Dmitrievich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yury of Zvenigorod was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> and prince of Zvenigorod and Galich. When his brother Vasily I died in 1425, he claimed the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> against his nephew Vasily II under the old rule of succession from brother to brother, opening a long civil war. He twice seized Moscow, in 1433 and 1434, and died there as grand prince in 1434.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yury-of-zvenigorod.jpg" alt="Yury of Zvenigorod" width="320" height="661" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian painter (1670s-1917) Public domain image (according to PD-Russia-expired) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frescos_in_Cathedral_of_the_Archangel_in_Moscow_-_west_wall_01_-_Yuri_of_Zvenigorod.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yury Dmitrievich, also known as George II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>. At his father's death he received in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> the towns of Zvenigorod, Ruza and Galich, and he was Duke of Zvenigorod and Galich from 1389 until his own death. During the reign of his brother Vasily I he took part in campaigns against Torzhok in 1392, Zhukotin in 1414 and Novgorod in 1417. By his wife Anastasia, daughter of Yury of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, he had three sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-kosoy">Vasily Kosoy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a> and Dmitry Krasny; the marriage made him brother-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Švitrigaila</a>, Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Besides the old house law of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik</a>, Yury read in his favour the testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>, written when Vasily I had been unmarried and childless. In 1430 uncle and nephew agreed to take the dispute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. The khan backed Vasily II but allowed Yury the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of his late younger brother, Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitrov">Dmitrov</a>. Vasily seized Dmitrov by force in 1432 and expelled Yury's governor, and Yury advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>, defeated Vasily on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klyazma">Klyazma River</a> and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, proclaiming himself grand duke.</p><p>Instead of imprisoning his nephew, Yury let him rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>. Muscovite boyars and noblemen flocked to Vasily's court there, and Yury concluded a treaty by which he renounced his claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, promised to deny help to his sons and exchanged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitrov">Dmitrov</a> for other lands, withdrawing to Galich. The following year Kosoy and Shemyaka defeated Vasily on the Kus River, and Vasily, told that Yury's troops had been among his enemies, set Galich ablaze in winter 1434, while Yury escaped north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belozersk">Belozersk</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 16 March 1434 the armies of uncle and nephew met between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl</a>, and Vasily's was heavily defeated; he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>. On 31 March, Wednesday of Bright Week, Yury entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> again and captured Vasily's family and treasury. He was preparing a new campaign when he died suddenly on 5 July 1434, and his eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-kosoy">Vasily Kosoy</a>, succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yury of Zvenigorod” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The principle was tested in the civil war of 1425–1453, when Vasily II fought his uncle <b>Yury of Zvenigorod</b> and his cousins Vasily Kosoy and Dmitry Shemyaka; Vasily was captured by the Tatars of Ulugh Muhammad in 1445 and blinded by his rivals in 1446, yet regained the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.74,38.86&amp;year=1413" aria-label="Pereslavl-Zalessky on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5389 237 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yury of Zvenigorod"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5839 507h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5839 507h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.74,38.86&amp;year=1413">What was here in 1413? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
